n  wrong science has been wrong governments  have lied etc what's the best way to  kind of handle that idea and approach  that  yeah i think i tell them and i have a  lot of criminal justice majors but i  think in general treat this like you  would if you're on a jury  so you have a burden of proof but one  thing you're never going to have is 100  certainty so you have to reach that  level to say is it 51 enough do i need  more  um so that's how you're measuring this  um and also we were just talking about  this uh this week um evidence evolves  um you have examples where you know in  in some of these you know conspiracies  you're gonna have revelations that might  take decades  or technology will change and you'll  have better ways to analyze something  so  patience is also a virtue for both of  those things and that's again you're  trying to teach americans patience  that's an ongoing enterprise  do you address the idea of sometimes  one might assert a certain belief not  because they actually believe it but  because it signals that they're part of  a team and i'll give an example of that  you know after the 2020 election there  was polling done about do you believe  trump won  and the numbers among republicans at the  time were quite high i think in the  immediate aftermath it was like 57 and  that number has waned over time but  within that 57 percent  presumably there were some what we might  call true believers who literally  believe trump actually won but then  there are others for whom claiming it  signals support for trump or or  identifies them with the team for lack  of a better term  where does that figure in to the studies  and the work that you do  i think i've used a lot of polling and a  lot of analysis of polling especially by  an author named berensky  you have you know influencers i mean  that's a new term but you've always had  people who lead opinions because they  they're trusted they have agency they're  public officials  um so people tend to 