n  wrote that there's a sort of uh  naivete in thinking that with any  platform or technology  you can eliminate any of the bad uses of  it and only be left with the sort of  good and i'm oversimplifying but the  idea that a platform a technology comes  with good and bad and the question is  finding a balance of sort of regulation  and education  that will maximize the good and minimize  the bad we can think about lots of great  things about social media and also it  has made the spread of conspiracy  theories and false beliefs rampant like  wildfire  what should be  a regulatory framework for some of these  platforms  that's a big that's a big question um  so best example i have online is a  website called metabunk which has  moderated conversations  um they do have standards and it it  works  um but how far that could be extended  and spread you know throughout the  entire  internet that's you know that's that's  the problem um  and i think you pointed out i mean where  these things might appear in places you  would have never suspected  uh the natural parenting community in  cuban i mean no one saw that  although you could see the elements  after the fact so  it's a it's a very hard thing to talk  about because the more you crank down on  free speech the more that you're going  to have the reaction and  i think it becomes self-defeating after  a point um  i'm back to trying to you know the  education part before they log on i mean  that's that's what i'm looking at that  that's i mean for me the greatest  immunizer to people falling for this  stuff is  you start teaching critical thinking and  media literacy  probably at age 11 or something like  that right  well and judging by the this course has  changed since i started you know back in  2016. i had 25 students right now i have  145. wow so i this thing get it it fills  in within a couple days every time i run  it and i run multiple sections now so  it might be like a factory for reason  and rational thinking i